## What this licence does and does not cover

This is the important part, and it is easy to get wrong.

**What the project can license.** The selection, curation and arrangement of
the catalogue, and the fields produced by this project's own work — the
`genre` bucketing, `type_game`, `safe`, `anti_cheat` and `is_kernel_ac`
judgements, `notes`, and the derived tables in `games/**`. That editorial
effort is the project's contribution and is what CC BY 4.0 is being applied to.

**What the project does NOT own, and therefore cannot license:**

- **`description`** — the opening of the short blurb each publisher writes for
  its own Steam store page. **Truncated to 180 characters**, on a word boundary
  where one is available, with an ellipsis marking the cut. 2,156 of 3,426 rows
  (63%) were shortened; the rest were already under the cap and are stored
  whole. No stored value exceeds 180 characters. Before truncation the
  median was 213 characters and the longest 340 — long enough that the field
  reproduced whole blurbs rather than quoting from them, which is the practice
  this cap exists to end. What remains is a short identifying quotation so a
  row is recognisable; copyright remains with the publisher, and it is
  **excluded** from the CC BY grant.
- **`header_image`** — a URL pointing at publisher artwork hosted on Valve's
  CDN. The dataset contains the address, never the image. The artwork itself is
  the publisher's.
- **Game names, and Steam's own tag vocabulary** — names are trademarks of
  their respective owners and appear here nominatively, to identify the game.
- **Anything reachable through `link`** — that is Valve's page, not ours.

**Facts are not owned by anyone.** Player counts, review percentages, release
dates, platform and language support are facts. The CC BY grant applies to this
project's compilation of them, not to the underlying facts, which nobody can
license to you.

**EU database rights.** Where a *sui generis* database right subsists in this
compilation, it is licensed on the same CC BY 4.0 terms.

## If you republish this data

Attribute the project, keep the caveats in
[docs/DISCLAIMER.md](./docs/DISCLAIMER.md) visible to your users, and do not
present the derived fields as authoritative — `genre`, `safe` and the
anti-cheat flags are one person's best effort, not an audit. Re-deriving
`description` from Steam yourself is safer than inheriting it from here.

## Not legal advice

This file was written by the maintainer, who is not a lawyer. It reflects a
good-faith engineering judgement about what can honestly be licensed, made so
that anyone reusing the data knows exactly where the boundaries are rather than
finding a blanket MIT notice over material the project never owned. If you need
certainty for a commercial use, get your own advice.
